{"causes": ["The ongoing Hundred Years' War created a political landscape where powerful French magnates like Charles I of Bourbon held significant military and diplomatic leverage.", "The inheritance of vast ducal territories, including Bourbon and Auvergne, provided Charles with immense wealth, prestige, and military resources.", "The internal struggles within the French nobility, such as the Praguerie revolt, positioned Charles as a key player in challenging or supporting the Valois monarchy."], "effects": ["Charles I of Bourbon's military service contributed significantly to the French reconquest of Normandy and Guyenne, leading to the end of the Hundred Years' War.", "His reconciliation with Charles VII helped to consolidate royal power and reduce the internal divisions that plagued France during the 15th century.", "The continued prominence of the House of Bourbon through Charles's descendants laid the groundwork for their future ascension to the French throne in the 16th century."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "charles-i-of-bourbon", "sourceName": "Charles I of Bourbon", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "hundred-years-war", "targetName": "Hundred Years' War", "context": "Charles I of Bourbon was a key commander in the final phases of the Hundred Years' War, leading French armies against the English between 1449 and 1453."}, {"sourceSlug": "charles-vii-of-france", "sourceName": "Charles VII of France", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "charles-i-of-bourbon", "targetName": "Charles I of Bourbon", "context": "Charles VII's efforts to centralize royal power initially led Charles I of Bourbon to participate in the Praguerie revolt against the king in 1440."}, {"sourceSlug": "charles-i-of-bourbon", "sourceName": "Charles I of Bourbon", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "duchy-of-bourbon", "targetName": "Duchy of Bourbon", "context": "Charles I of Bourbon ruled and derived his primary title from the Duchy of Bourbon, a significant territory in central France, from 1434 until his death."}, {"sourceSlug": "charles-i-of-bourbon", "sourceName": "Charles I of Bourbon", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "charles-vii-of-france", "targetName": "Charles VII of France", "context": "After their reconciliation, Charles I of Bourbon became a loyal and effective military commander for Charles VII, notably during the reconquest of Normandy and Guyenne."}, {"sourceSlug": "battle-of-castillon", "sourceName": "Battle of Castillon", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "charles-i-of-bourbon", "targetName": "Charles I of Bourbon", "context": "The Battle of Castillon in 1453, which effectively ended the Hundred Years' War, occurred during Charles I of Bourbon's period of active military command for France."}], "places": [{"name": "Moulins, France", "role": "Ducal Residence"}, {"name": "Bordeaux, France", "role": "Reconquest Target"}, {"name": "Orléans, France", "role": "Strategic City"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Charles I of Bourbon", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "charles-i-of-bourbon"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q692291", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_I,_Duke_of_Bourbon"], "tags": ["France", "Military Leaders & Commanders", "Europe", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Charles I of Bourbon was a significant regional magnate whose military leadership directly contributed to the French victory in the Hundred Years' War, thereby strengthening the Valois monarchy and shaping the future of the French state. His family's subsequent rise to the French throne further underscores the long-term impact of his lineage and political maneuvering.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-27T08:17:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45963988726", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Charles de Bourbon was the oldest son of John I, Duke of Bourbon, and Marie, Duchess of Auvergne.", "newValue": "Charles I, Duke of Bourbon and Auvergne (1434-1456), was a powerful French magnate whose strategic shifts and military service were pivotal in the final, decisive phases of the Hundred Years' War. Born into the influential House of Bourbon, a cadet branch of the Capetian dynasty, Charles inherited vast territories in central France, making him one of the wealthiest and most militarily significant feudal lords of his era. His political maneuvering, initially against King Charles VII, and subsequent loyal service, profoundly impacted the consolidation of royal power and the expulsion of English forces from continental France.

Succeeding his father, John I, Duke of Bourbon, in 1434, Charles initially allied with other powerful nobles in the Praguerie revolt of 1440 against Charles VII, seeking to limit royal authority. However, he soon reconciled with the Crown and became a trusted commander. Charles played a crucial role in the reconquest of Normandy (1449-1450) and Guyenne (1451-1453), leading royal armies in key campaigns that culminated in the Battle of Castillon (1453), effectively ending the Hundred Years' War. His marriage to Agnes of Burgundy, daughter of John the Fearless, further cemented complex aristocratic alliances and rivalries of the period.

His contributions helped solidify the French monarchy's authority after generations of conflict, laying groundwork for the centralizing policies of subsequent Valois kings. The House of Bourbon, through Charles's lineage, continued to be a dominant force in French politics, eventually ascending to the throne with Henry IV in 1589, demonstrating the long-term dynastic consequences of the medieval ducal power he wielded. Charles's strategic choices and military leadership were instrumental in transforming France from a fractured kingdom into a more unified state."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-27T08:17:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45963988726", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1401"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1401"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Hundred Years' War created a political landscape where powerful French magnates like Charles I of Bourbon held significant military and diplomatic leverage.", "The inheritance of vast ducal territories, including Bourbon and Auvergne, provided Charles with immense wealth, prestige, and military resources.", "The internal struggles within the French nobility, such as the Praguerie revolt, positioned Charles as a key player in challenging or supporting the Valois monarchy."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-27T08:17:34Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45963988726", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1456-12-13"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1456-12-13"}], "newValue": ["Charles I of Bourbon's military service contributed significantly to the French reconquest of Normandy and Guyenne, leading to the e